  "query_preview": "..\n\nFriday through Monday: An upper level low pressure system will spin \nover the Inland Northwest keeping temperatures seasonably cool and \nthe weather active. The center of the low is situated over northwest \nOregon. A deformation band forming on the eastern flank will swing \nacross the Idaho Panhandle through early Friday morning. \nPeriods of light to moderate rain will soak the ground from the \nLewiston- Clarkston Valley up across the Palouse region, and \nacross the southern to central Idaho Panhandle. Total \nprecipitation under this band will be up to between 0.50-1.0 \ninches across much of this area. Much needed rainfall after a \nwarmer and drier than average June- August. Steady rain will \ntransition to showers for Friday afternoon. Showers will favor \nthe higher terrain, but even the Columbia Basin will see at \nleast isolated showers. Weak surface based instability over the \nCascades and the mountains of north-central Washington will \nbring an isolated threat for thunderstorms. Any storms that \ndevelop this afternoon will be capable of brief heavy rain, and\ninfrequent lightning strikes.\n\nThe center of the upper level low wobbles around over the Northwest \nRegion through at least Sunday. This will keep the region \nconditionally unstable. Best chances for showers will come in the \nafternoon with diurnally forced showers. Perturbations that \nspin around the upper low will also help to force precipitation.\nThe next one looks to be around Saturday afternoon with more \norganized development of showers across extreme eastern \nWashington into the Idaho Panhandle. Then another round with \nanother shortwave spinning in across these same areas for Sunday\nnight into Monday. It's really tough to pin point how much \nprecipitation will fall as there will be some convective \nelements in place with modestly steep lapse rates at mid levels \nof the atmosphere. There will be the potential for heavier \nshowers that brings wetting rains (greater than a tenth of an \ninch), and this will all largely be beneficial for the ongoing \ndrought across the region. Where rain may be of a nuisance is if\nit gets heavy over a recent burn scar, and that would bring a \nsmall chance for minor flooding impacts.\n\nThe upper level low gets picked up by the Polar Jet stream around \nlate Sunday into Monday. A 30-60% chance of precipitation will \ncontinue across the eastern half of the region while the Cascades \nbegins to dry out. Temperatures will be below normal with highs \ngenerally in the upper 60s to mid 70s through Labor Day Weekend. \nBreaks in the cloud cover overnight may yield patchy fog as rain \nwill moisten up the boundary layer. The next few days is a reminder \nthat the fall season is right around the corner.\n\nMonday night through Friday: The Inland Northwest remains under a \nbaggy longwave trough of low pressure into next week. A longwave \nhigh pressure area will be situated over the central portion of the \nU.S. The trough axis will be situated more offshore and it looks to \ndig around the Tuesday into Wednesday timeframe. The equatorial \nPacific is also quite active with tropical systems, and there's some \nindication that one of these systems could get entrained into the \nmid latitudes off of the coast of Baja California by Wednesday. This \ntends to muck up the models with reduced confidence in the extended \nforecast. The general consensus, however, is that the ridging in the \ncentral U.S. will build in toward the Inland Northwest with a \nwarming and drying trend around mid week. We will need to keep an \neye on that tropical moisture for later in the week though as \nthere is a possibility for some of that to get entrained and \nadvect northward into the region. Early indications is that if \nthis happens, it will be a small fraction and won't be more than\na minor impact. /SVH",
